{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-91686",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write $1\\,\\frac{3}{5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{(1\\times 5)+3}{5}=\\frac{8}{5}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+3}{4}=\\frac{11}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{5}\\times \\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{5}\\times \\frac{11}{4} & =\\frac{8}{4}\\times \\frac{11}{5} \\\\  & =2\\times \\frac{11}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $22.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
